    {
      "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
      "source": "pdf_layout",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "We define and analyze the Commutative Isogeny Hidden Number Problem which is the natural analogue of the Hidden Num- ber Problem in the CSIDH and CSURF setting. In short, the task is as follows: Given two supersingular elliptic curves E A, E B and access to an oracle that outputs some of the most significant bits of the CDH of two curves, an adversary must compute the shared curve E AB = CDH(E A ,E B). We show that we can recover E AB in polynomial time by using Cop- persmith'smethodaslongastheoracleoutputs 13+ε≈54%(CSIDH) 24 and 31 +ε ≈ 76% (CSURF) of the most significant bits of the CDH, 41 where ε > 0 is an arbitrarily small constant. To this end, we give a purely combinatorial restatement of Coppersmith's method, effectively concealing the intricate aspects of lattice theory and allowing for near- complete automation. By leveraging this approach, we attain recovery attacks with ε close to zero within a few minutes of computation. · · · ·",
      "extractor": "pdfplumber_section_boundary"
    },
